"version: powerfarm.conformance/v0\npurpose: Behavioral cases that implementations should turn into executable tests. Technology choices are intentionally absent.\ncases:\n  - id: APP-001\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Reject filesystem traversal in a declared store locator\n    given: An App Contract contains stateStores[].locator.relativePath with ../.\n    expect: Schema validation fails and no materialization begins.\n\n  - id: APP-002\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Physical placement alone does not admit an application\n    given: Application bytes exist under an App Park but no recognized App Contract generation and admission evidence exist.\n    expect: The application is not institutionally admitted.\n\n  - id: APP-003\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Missing required admission evidence prevents recognition\n    given: Materialization completes only a subset of spec.admission requirements.\n    expect: The candidate may remain staged or partial but MUST NOT become a recognized active App Contract generation.\n\n  - id: APP-004\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Re-admitting the same immutable generation is idempotent\n    given: The same contract id, generation, and contract digest are submitted twice.\n    expect: The second operation returns the existing recognition or equivalent idempotent success without creating a competing generation.\n\n  - id: APP-005\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Conflicting bytes under one generation are rejected\n    given: An existing contract id and generation is recognized with digest A and a candidate arrives with digest B.\n    expect: Recognition is rejected; a changed contract requires a new generation.\n\n  - id: APP-006\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Existing store is never silently recreated\n    given: Admission finds a declared store at the expected locator with a schema identity different from the contract.\n    expect: Materialization stops for migration or operator decision and preserves the existing bytes.\n\n  - id: APP-007\n    area: app-contract\n    title: Live database file digest is not persistent store identity\n    given: A conforming local database changes rows while its store contract and schema generation remain unchanged.\n    expect: The store remains the same institutional store; only explicit immutable snapshots receive content digests.\n\n  - id: APP-008\n    area: search\n    title: Search discovers stores from recognized contracts\n    given: A store is searchable in local files but absent from recognized App Contracts.\n    expect: Powerfarm Search does not treat it as a recognized authoritative source merely because it is reachable.\n\n  - id: REG-001\n    area: registry\n    title: Content existence does not imply recognition\n    given: A valid CAS object exists with a known digest but no Registry artifact version or contract refers to it.\n    expect: The object has material identity but no institutional meaning solely from existence.\n\n  - id: REG-002\n    area: registry\n    title: Digest mismatch rejects content resolution\n    given: A ContentRef expects digest A but the fetched bytes hash to B.\n    expect: Resolution fails and the bytes are not returned as object A.\n\n  - id: REG-003\n    area: registry\n    title: Supersession preserves prior contract generation\n    given: Contract generation 2 supersedes generation 1.\n    expect: Generation 1 remains historically addressable and generation 2 is the current recognized generation.\n\n  - id: REG-004\n    area: registry\n    title: Grant revocation preserves history\n    given: A current grant is revoked.\n    expect: The grant remains addressable with revocation time and reason; it is not deleted to represent revocation.\n\n  - id: REG-005\n    area: registry\n    title: Search index cannot grant authority\n    given: A Search projection contains a stale record not present in current Registry recognition.\n    expect: Search may return the stale projection with provenance but MUST NOT elevate it to current institutional authority.\n\n  - id: EXEC-001\n    area: executability\n    title: Unknown evidence is not true\n    given: T evaluates true and O evaluates unknown.\n    expect: Ready is not satisfied and no trigger is produced.\n\n  - id: EXEC-002\n    area: executability\n    title: Scope mismatch cannot create readiness\n    given: Temporal evidence belongs to activation scope A and observational evidence belongs to unrelated scope B.\n    expect: The evidence cannot be combined into one Ready assertion.\n\n  - id: EXEC-003\n    area: executability\n    title: Observation before temporal evidence may be retained\n    given: O becomes true first and policy observationBeforeTemporal is retain; T later becomes true within validity limits.\n    expect: Readiness may become true using the retained observation if it remains valid.\n\n  - id: EXEC-004\n    area: executability\n    title: Observation before temporal evidence may be discarded\n    given: O becomes true first and policy observationBeforeTemporal is discard; T later becomes true.\n    expect: The old observation does not satisfy O for that activation; new admissible observational evidence is required.\n\n  - id: EXEC-005\n    area: executability\n    title: Temporal evidence before observation may be retained\n    given: T becomes true first and policy temporalBeforeObservation is retain; O later becomes true within the temporal window.\n    expect: Readiness may become true for the same activation.\n\n  - id: EXEC-006\n    area: executability\n    title: Exclusive claim has one winner\n    given: Two workers observe the same Trigger for the same contract id, generation, and activation id.\n    expect: At most one valid exclusive claim is acquired.\n\n  - id: EXEC-007\n    area: executability\n    title: Crash after claim but before dispatch is recoverable\n    given: A worker acquires a claim and crashes before the first effect dispatch.\n    expect: Recovery can safely resume or reacquire according to claim policy without fabricating a completed effect.\n\n  - id: EXEC-008\n    area: executability\n    title: Reconcilable effect is verified before retry after uncertainty\n    given: Dispatch may have reached the target, the worker crashes before outcome certainty, and the effect class is reconcilable.\n    expect: State becomes uncertain and verification/reconciliation occurs before any repeat dispatch.\n\n  - id: EXEC-009\n    area: executability\n    title: Idempotent retry preserves logical idempotency key\n    given: An idempotent effect is retried for the same activation after a transient failure.\n    expect: Retries use the same logical idempotency key and a new attempt identity.\n\n  - id: EXEC-010\n    area: executability\n    title: Irreversible uncertain effect is not blindly replayed\n    given: Dispatch of an irreversible effect has unknown outcome.\n    expect: Automatic repeat dispatch is forbidden; the state remains uncertain pending explicit reconciliation or policy.\n\n  - id: EXEC-011\n    area: executability\n    title: Transport success does not imply verified effect\n    given: Dispatch returns HTTP 200 and post-effect verification evaluates false.\n    expect: The activation is not marked verified or done solely because of the HTTP response.\n\n  - id: EXEC-012\n    area: executability\n    title: Expiration before claim prevents materialization\n    given: Readiness occurred but the expiration guard became true before a valid claim was acquired.\n    expect: No new effect materialization begins for that expired activation.\n\n  - id: EXEC-013\n    area: executability\n    title: Cancellation does not erase already-dispatched history\n    given: An activation is cancelled after a dispatch has occurred.\n    expect: Cancellation stops future permitted work while preserving dispatch and certainty history for reconciliation.\n\n  - id: EXEC-014\n    area: executability\n    title: Supersession does not rewrite an in-flight generation\n    given: Generation 2 is recognized while an activation of generation 1 is already claimed.\n    expect: The in-flight activation retains generation 1 semantics unless an explicit cancellation/supersession policy says otherwise.\n\n  - id: EXEC-015\n    area: executability\n    title: Recurring temporal occurrences have distinct activation identities\n    given: A recurrence becomes due on two separate occurrences.\n    expect: Each intended occurrence has a distinct activation id while retries of one occurrence retain its activation id.\n\n  - id: EXEC-016\n    area: executability\n    title: Level trigger does not busy-loop by itself\n    given: T and O remain continuously true under level-triggered semantics.\n    expect: Retrigger behavior is controlled by policy/history; mere repeated evaluation MUST NOT create unbounded duplicate activations.\n\n  - id: OBS-001\n    area: observability\n    title: Heartbeat and census retain separate provenance\n    given: A fresh spontaneous heartbeat exists but the most recent scheduled census failed.\n    expect: A projection may show the discrepancy; it does not collapse both observations into one opaque health fact.\n\n  - id: OBS-002\n    area: observability\n    title: Unrecognized speaker remains unrecognized\n    given: Antenna receives valid signals from an entity absent from current Registry recognition.\n    expect: The arrival is preserved as observational evidence but does not create institutional identity or authority.\n\n  - id: HEART-001\n    area: heartime\n    title: Planning review precedes coverage expiry\n    given: A responsibility enters period N with valid coverage.\n    expect: A review is durable before expiry and a successful renewal atomically arms period N+1.\n  - id: HEART-002\n    area: heartime\n    title: Failed planning has explicit fallback\n    given: Coverage expires without a valid renewal.\n    expect: Ordinary work is suppressed, contractual fallback is emitted, and another review remains due.\n  - id: HEART-003\n    area: heartime\n    title: Kill after occurrence commit\n    given: SIGKILL occurs after occurrence/evidence/outbox/next deadline commit and before publication.\n    expect: Restart exposes the same occurrence and pending delivery with no duplicate semantic identity.\n  - id: HEART-004\n    area: heartime\n    title: Contractual catch-up\n    given: Several nominal instants pass during downtime under all, latest, and skip contracts.\n    expect: All retains bounded batches without losing backlog, latest records coalesced coverage, and skip records omissions.\n  - id: HEART-005\n    area: heartime\n    title: Contractual overlap\n    given: Prior published work remains unresolved when another occurrence is due.\n    expect: Allow, defer and coalesce control publication as declared without treating silence as completion.\n  - id: HEART-006\n    area: heartime\n    title: Acknowledgement is not verification\n    given: Delivery returns success and the process restarts before execution evidence arrives.\n    expect: Status reports possibly executed and unresolved; a return deadline remains and no effect success is invented.\n  - id: HEART-007\n    area: heartime\n    title: Pause and supersession preserve coverage\n    given: An obligation is paused or replaced while a published occurrence is unresolved.\n    expect: Its resume/review remains durable and historical occurrences retain their original terms.\n  - id: ATTN-001\n    area: attention\n    title: Mandatory context does not compete for salience\n    given: The context budget cannot fit authority and restrictions.\n    expect: Compilation fails rather than omitting mandatory terms.\n  - id: ATTN-002\n    area: attention\n    title: Card expiry does not retire responsibility\n    given: A discrepancy Card expires before its underlying failure is resolved.\n    expect: Evidence, failure history and next temporal evaluation remain addressable.\n  - id: TURN-001\n    area: continuity\n    title: Independent intelligence succession\n    given: Occupant A changes durable institutional artifacts, then a fresh occupant B receives only a new WakePack and referenced state.\n    expect: B continues and produces independently verified work without A conversation or narrative handoff.\n  - id: TURN-002\n    area: recovery\n    title: Technical recovery does not require human engineering\n    given: The primary executor fails under a bounded recovery contract.\n    expect: Diagnosis, authorized fallback or containment is recorded with finite limits and future evaluation.\n  - id: TURN-003\n    area: recovery\n    title: Genuine Direction boundary\n    given: No authorized technical route can resolve a material Direction or legitimacy choice.\n    expect: A record identifies what happened, consequence, attempted recovery and exact decision without asking for terminal debugging.\n  - id: CENSUS-001\n    area: census\n    title: Cohort and mismatch classes are reconstructable\n    given: Recognized population changes during a sweep with known, misplaced, absent, unknown, unrecognized and explicitly prohibited observations.\n    expect: The frozen cohort governs that sweep; classes remain distinct and no repair authority follows from detection.\n"

